<Layout
  title="Understanding Hazard Pointers — Harsh"
  description="What I learned while building a lightweight hazard-pointer library in Rust."
>
  <article class="article">

    <a class="back" href="/">← back to writing</a>

    <header class="article-header">
      <div class="eyebrow">Rust · Concurrency · Memory</div>

      <h1>Understanding Hazard Pointers</h1>

      <div class="article-meta">
        September 18, 2026 · 8 min read
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="article-content">

      <p>
        Hazard pointers are one of those things that look terrifying
        until you understand the problem they're actually solving.
      </p>

      <p>
        I started learning about them while working on
        <a href="https://github.com/harshsys0x/haphazard">
          haphazard
        </a>,
        a small Rust library for hazard-pointer based memory reclamation.
      </p>

      <h2>The problem</h2>

      <p>
        Imagine a lock-free data structure where one thread is reading
        an object while another thread removes it.
      </p>

      <pre><code>{`Thread A                  Thread B

read pointer ────────┐
                     │
                     │    remove object
                     │
                     │    free(object)
                     │
use pointer ─────────┘
                     ↑
                  use-after-free`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        The pointer itself may still contain a perfectly valid-looking
        address. The memory behind that address is the problem.
      </p>

      <h2>What a hazard pointer does</h2>

      <p>
        A hazard pointer is essentially a public announcement:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        "I'm currently using this object. Please don't free it."
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        A thread publishes the address it is about to dereference.
        A thread removing objects can then check those published
        addresses before reclaiming memory.
      </p>

      <h2>The basic idea</h2>

      <pre><code>{`protect(pointer)

    ↓

publish pointer

    ↓

check that pointer is still valid

    ↓

use object safely

    ↓

clear hazard pointer`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        The interesting part isn't the API.
        The interesting part is making those steps work correctly
        when multiple CPUs are changing things at the same time.
      </p>

      <h2>Why Rust makes this interesting</h2>

      <p>
        Rust already gives us powerful guarantees around references,
        ownership and lifetimes.
      </p>

      <p>
        Hazard pointers live in a world where those guarantees aren't
        enough by themselves. We are dealing with raw pointers,
        atomics, reclamation and carefully controlled unsafe code.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's where the learning gets interesting.
      </p>

      <h2>What I'm still figuring out</h2>

      <p>
        The more I work on this, the more I realize that lock-free
        programming isn't mainly about writing clever atomic operations.
      </p>

      <p>
        It's about understanding exactly what every thread is allowed
        to observe, when those observations become visible, and when
        memory can actually be reclaimed.
      </p>

      <p>
        This post is the beginning of that exploration.
      </p>

    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>